\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Final technical frame of the historical video embedded in the source presentation: Markl's operating equation is written as $iM_E/Z \le (f=6/N^{0.2}\le 1.0)(1.25S_c+0.25S_h)$, i.e.\ the thermal-expansion moment range is limited by the fatigue-cycle factor $f$ with an upper cap at 1.0. The accompanying bullets explain that the cap prevents the allowable stress range from increasing any further below about 7000 cycles, and that the $1.25S_c+0.25S_h$ form is intended to prevent ratcheting after deducting the pressure-plus-deadweight share already assumed to consume the hot allowable.}}{6}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:markl-equation-frame}{{6}{6}{Final technical frame of the historical video embedded in the source presentation: Markl's operating equation is written as $iM_E/Z \le (f=6/N^{0.2}\le 1.0)(1.25S_c+0.25S_h)$, i.e.\ the thermal-expansion moment range is limited by the fatigue-cycle factor $f$ with an upper cap at 1.0. The accompanying bullets explain that the cap prevents the allowable stress range from increasing any further below about 7000 cycles, and that the $1.25S_c+0.25S_h$ form is intended to prevent ratcheting after deducting the pressure-plus-deadweight share already assumed to consume the hot allowable}{figure.caption.8}{}}
